{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# `tool_step_limit` empty-filter cutoff 400s on Bedrock/Anthropic — forced landing is provider-broken\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nLIVE-proven (Bedrock us-east-1, haiku-4.5): `build_agent_kwargs` (`src/rebar/llm/agent_call.py:64`)\nimplements `tool_step_limit` by rewriting toolsets to `.filtered(lambda ctx, _: ctx.run_step <= limit)`.\nWhen the filter trips after any tool call has occurred, pydantic-ai omits `tools`/`toolConfig` from the\nnext request while the message history still contains `toolUse` blocks. Bedrock Converse rejects that\nrequest: ValidationException 400 \"The toolConfig field must be defined when using toolUse and toolResult\ncontent blocks.\" Anthropic direct has the equivalent documented constraint (tools must be defined when\nhistory contains `tool_use`), and pydantic-ai maps `tool_choice='none'` on Bedrock to omitting\n`toolConfig` entirely — the same 400. So the convergence boundary NEVER lands a run that has already\nmade a tool call — it converts it into a provider error, which `interpret_failure`\n(`src/rebar/llm/structured_run.py:58`) then sweeps into `LLMUnavailableError` (\"provider call failed\").\n\nBlast radius: every armed site. `src/rebar/llm/workflow/completion_recovery.py:471` passes\n`tool_step_limit=_EVIDENCE_TOOL_STEPS` (16) — the bounded-recovery fallback itself dies with the same\n400 if an evidence run reaches step 17.\n\nWhy tests miss it: `tests/unit/test_agent_call_seam.py` and `tests/unit/test_runner_pydantic_budget.py`\ndrive the cutoff with `FunctionModel`, which accepts a tools-less request over a `tool_use` history;\nthe provider protocol constraint is unrepresented offline.\n\n## Approach\n\nKeep the `tool_step_limit` request field and its meaning (a convergence boundary after N run steps) but\nchange the mechanism from *hiding tools* to *steering with synthetic tool results*: wrap the toolsets in\na `WrapperToolset` subclass whose `call_tool`, once `run_ctx.run_step > limit`, stops executing the tool\nand instead returns a fixed steering notice telling the model that tool results are no longer provided\nand it must produce its final answer from the evidence already gathered. Tool definitions stay advertised\nfor the whole run, so the request is always protocol-valid on both first-class providers; nothing raises\nmid-protocol; the model lands naturally through the existing output path. LIVE-proven end to end: a\ndeliberately tool-happy agent landed cleanly after 3 real + 7 steered calls, and a structured\n`completion_verdict` parsed through the prompted-output path after 4 real + 2 steered calls\n(experiments exp_g/exp_h, recorded on [rebar:c827-2a23-cd00-473e] 2026-08-05). This fits the problem\nbecause it is the one shape that keeps the Bedrock/Anthropic constraint satisfied by construction —\nindustry equivalents (LangChain `early_stopping_method=\"generate\"`, smolagents `provide_final_answer`,\nCrewAI `handle_max_iterations_exceeded`) all converge on \"stop executing, force a final answer\" rather\nthan \"remove the tools\"; pydantic-ai itself offers no graceful-landing primitive (issue #2605 open).\n\n`FunctionToolset` and `WrapperToolset` are both importable from `pydantic_ai.toolsets` (confirmed in the\ninstalled pydantic-ai 1.107.1: `pydantic_ai/toolsets/__init__.py` exports both; `WrapperToolset.call_tool`\nis the documented override seam). The steering text is a module constant beside the wrapper.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/agent_call.py` — replace the `.filtered()` empty-cutoff branch in `build_agent_kwargs`\n  with the steering wrapper; module constant for the steering text.\n- `tests/unit/test_agent_call_seam.py` — rewrite the cutoff-shape tests to pin the new invariant (tool\n  definitions persist past the limit; execution is steered instead).\n- `tests/unit/test_runner_pydantic_budget.py` — update the truncation test to assert steering semantics\n  through the runner.\n\nOut of scope: the runaway detector and its trip wiring ([rebar:c827-2a23-cd00-473e]); the recovery\ncatch-miss ([rebar:fd84-92b4-01ba-4911]). `completion_recovery.py:471` needs no edit — it passes\n`tool_step_limit` through `RunRequest`, and this ticket fixes what that field does.\n\n## Testing\n\n    pytest tests/unit/test_agent_call_seam.py tests/unit/test_runner_pydantic_budget.py -q\n\nHappy path: a run under the limit executes tools normally and lands its output. Edge: a run that\nexceeds the limit gets steering results, keeps its tool definitions advertised on every request, and\nstill lands. Failure-shape regression: a test asserts that after the limit trips, the toolset still\nadvertises non-empty tool definitions (the exact condition whose violation produced the 400) — the\nassertion is on the request-visible tool surface, not on implementation internals.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] no code path empties the advertised tool surface mid-run after a tool call has occurred: a test\n      drives a `FunctionModel` run past the limit and asserts every request's tool definitions remain\n      non-empty for the whole run\n- [ ] past the limit, tool calls are not executed — the tool returns the steering notice instead; a test\n      asserts the underlying tool function is not invoked after the limit and the model receives the\n      steering text as the tool result\n- [ ] a run that exceeds the limit still lands a final output through the normal output path (no\n      exception, no empty result); pinned by a `FunctionModel` test whose model calls tools until it\n      receives a steering result, then emits its answer\n- [ ] a run that never reaches the limit behaves exactly as before: tools execute, output lands; pinned\n      by the existing happy-path tests passing (updated only where they asserted the old filtered shape)\n- [ ] `tool_step_limit=0` and negative values steer from the first tool call (the existing clamp\n      semantics are preserved); verified by a test\n- [ ] `tests/unit/workflow/test_completion_token_recovery.py` still passes: the tool-free finalizer\n      (`tool_step_limit is None`, `mode=\"single_turn\"`) is untouched by this change"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785955004699787001,"uuid":"4c1cec4f-b3f6-4301-9886-df89677ef322"}